1
00:00:00,746 --> 00:00:14,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, today's sutta is Samyutta Nikaya 22.81, which is just titled as Parileya.

2
00:00:16,222 --> 00:00:23,069
[SPEAKER_00]: It starts by saying that the auspicious one was staying near Kosambi, in Gosita's monastery,

3
00:00:24,259 --> 00:00:28,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the morning, he dressed, took his ball and entered Kolsumbi for alms.

4
00:00:28,545 --> 00:00:34,973
[SPEAKER_00]: After the meal, on his return from alms round, he set his lodgings in order for himself.

5
00:00:35,093 --> 00:00:41,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking his ball and robe without informing his attendants of taking the leave of the Bhikkhu Saṅgha, he set out to go wandering alone with no companion.

6
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:49,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, not long after, the auspicious one left, one of the bhikkhus asked Ananda, well, told Ananda about the matter.

7
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:58,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Ananda said, when the auspicious one departs in this manner, it means he won't stay alone, and this time no one should go after him.

8
00:00:58,465 --> 00:01:05,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the auspicious one, traveling stage by stage, arrived eventually at Perilea, where he stayed at the root of a sacred Sal tree.

9
00:01:05,689 --> 00:01:12,092
[SPEAKER_00]: The several bhikkhus approached Ananda and exchanged pleasantries with him, and once the amicable and cordial talk was over,

10
00:01:13,075 --> 00:01:19,040
[SPEAKER_00]: They asked him... well, they told him that it's been a long time since they heard a talk from the auspicious one.

11
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,121
[SPEAKER_00]: They would like to hear a dhamma talk from him.

12
00:01:25,585 --> 00:01:33,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Ananda, together with those bhikkhus, went to Peralaya, where the Buddha was staying, bowed, sat down, and they asked him to give a dhamma talk.

13
00:01:35,412 --> 00:01:38,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm not gonna read every introduction part, a paragraph.

14
00:01:40,236 --> 00:01:42,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question they asked him was,

15
00:01:44,693 --> 00:01:45,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, sorry.

16
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:47,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, actually, one of the bhikkhus had a thought.

17
00:01:48,157 --> 00:01:54,604
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who knows and sees in which way is there an immediate destruction of influxes, of asavas.

18
00:01:56,337 --> 00:01:59,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the Buddha knew what that Bhikkhu was thinking.

19
00:02:00,841 --> 00:02:04,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he addressed them by saying, Bhikkhus, I've explained the dhamma thoroughly.

20
00:02:05,244 --> 00:02:17,774
[SPEAKER_00]: I've thoroughly explained the four establishments of recollectedness, the four right efforts, the four bases of spiritual ability, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the Noble Eightfold Path.

21
00:02:18,735 --> 00:02:20,516
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how I have explained the dhamma thoroughly.

22
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:36,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Though I've explained the dhamma thoroughly, still a certain bhikkhu present here has this thought, for one who knows and sees in which way is there immediate destruction of the influxes.

23
00:02:40,062 --> 00:02:41,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha answers it.

24
00:02:42,715 --> 00:02:58,260
[SPEAKER_00]: An unlearned, ordinary person, who has not seen the Noble Ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the Noble Ones, who has not seen the Righteous Men, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teachings of the Righteous Men, regards form as self.

25
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But that regarding is an activation, saṅkhara.

26
00:03:04,522 --> 00:03:10,624
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the foundation, the origin, the birthplace, and the source of that saṅkhara, of that activation?

27
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:16,883
[SPEAKER_00]: For an unlearned, ordinary person, struck by feelings born of ignorance, pressure, craving, has arisen.

28
00:03:18,944 --> 00:03:20,765
[SPEAKER_00]: That activation is born from that.

29
00:03:21,705 --> 00:03:27,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that activation is impermanent, determined, dependently originated.

30
00:03:27,888 --> 00:03:34,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And that craving too, that feeling, that pressure, that ignorance, are also impermanent, determined, and dependently originated.

31
00:03:35,132 --> 00:03:40,475
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who knows and sees in this way, because there is the immediate destruction of the influxes.

32
00:03:42,217 --> 00:03:47,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it repeats the same for other aggregates.

33
00:03:48,505 --> 00:03:54,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps they do not regard form as self, they still regard self as possessing of form.

34
00:03:55,889 --> 00:03:56,789
[SPEAKER_00]: That's an activation.

35
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:02,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps they do not regard form as self, or self as possessing form, but they still regard form in self.

36
00:04:03,633 --> 00:04:05,294
[SPEAKER_00]: But that regarding is an activation.

37
00:04:06,234 --> 00:04:13,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Or perhaps they do not regard form as self, or self as possessing form, or as form in self, but they still regard self in form.

38
00:04:14,765 --> 00:04:19,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whichever way you turn it around, there is self involved, there is the appropriation.

39
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:23,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it says, yeah, now it goes for the aggregated.

40
00:04:23,871 --> 00:04:39,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, perhaps, they don't regard form as self, self-possessing form, form in self, self in form, but they regard feeling as self, activations as self, consciousness as self, perception, activation, consciousness as self, but that regarding is an activation, and then repeats the same.

41
00:04:49,902 --> 00:05:04,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it says, or perhaps they do not regard form or feeling or perception or activations or consciousness as self, but they still have such a view that self and the cosmos are one and the same.

42
00:05:04,986 --> 00:05:09,368
[SPEAKER_00]: After passing away, I will be permanent, everlasting, eternal, imperishable.

43
00:05:10,388 --> 00:05:13,229
[SPEAKER_00]: But that eternalist view is an activation.

44
00:05:13,669 --> 00:05:15,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the foundation of that activation?

45
00:05:15,389 --> 00:05:16,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it repeats the same.

46
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:18,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the craving in regard to the feeling.

47
00:05:20,975 --> 00:05:33,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, perhaps, they do not regard form, or feeling, or perception, or activation, or consciousness as self, nor do they have such a view that self and cosmos are one and the same, and that after passing away, they will be permanent, everlasting, eternal, and imperishable.

48
00:05:34,146 --> 00:05:40,852
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, they have such a view that I might not be, and it might not be mine, I will not be, and it will not be mine.

49
00:05:42,006 --> 00:05:44,708
[SPEAKER_00]: But that annihilationist view is an activation.

50
00:05:45,108 --> 00:05:46,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the foundation of that activation?

51
00:05:47,029 --> 00:05:55,094
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who knows and sees in this way, I mean, repeats the same and then it finishes, but that the one who knows and sees in this way, there is the immediate destruction of influxes.

52
00:05:56,815 --> 00:06:03,980
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the last part is, or perhaps they do not regard form, or feeling, or perception, or activations, or consciousness as self.

53
00:06:04,661 --> 00:06:14,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor do they have such a view that self and the cosmos are one and the same, and after passing away, they will be permanent, everlasting, eternal, imperishable.

54
00:06:14,728 --> 00:06:19,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor do they have a view that I might not be, and it will not, it might not be mine.

55
00:06:19,832 --> 00:06:21,553
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not be, and it will not be mine.

56
00:06:22,314 --> 00:06:24,796
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, they have doubts and uncertainties.

57
00:06:25,685 --> 00:06:33,254
[SPEAKER_00]: They're undecided about the true dhamma, that doubt and uncertainty, that indecision about the true dhamma is an activation.

58
00:06:33,555 --> 00:06:35,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the foundation of that activation?

59
00:06:35,898 --> 00:06:41,204
[SPEAKER_00]: For an unlearned ordinary person struck by feelings born of ignorance pressure, craving has arisen.

60
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:55,462
[SPEAKER_00]: That activation is born from that, and that activation is impermanent, determined, dependently originated, and that craving too, that feeling, that pressure, passa, that ignorance are also impermanent, determined, and dependently originated.

61
00:06:56,363 --> 00:07:01,443
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who knows and sees in this way, that is the immediate destruction of the influxes.

62
00:07:03,644 --> 00:07:05,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's get back to the top.

63
00:07:07,124 --> 00:07:09,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so the obvious thing here is

64
00:07:12,422 --> 00:07:17,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much, obviously, it's the same core paragraph that repeats through the sutta.

65
00:07:18,528 --> 00:07:33,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a patujana, an unlearned ordinary person, has not seen the Noble Ones, is not trained in the teaching, has basically made no effort in taming the citta, doesn't even know what that entails, let alone practice it.

66
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:37,142
[SPEAKER_00]: He regards his form as self.

67
00:07:37,622 --> 00:07:50,337
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, anything that can appear, anything that is manifested, which is the five aggregates, he, one way or the other, as the rest of the sutra explains, he regards as self.

68
00:07:50,978 --> 00:07:51,859
[SPEAKER_00]: He appropriates it.

69
00:07:52,519 --> 00:07:53,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a matter of choice.

70
00:07:54,081 --> 00:07:55,162
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you can just

71
00:07:58,830 --> 00:08:05,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Learn the new things and then suddenly stop regarding form itself, stop regarding aggregates itself.

72
00:08:06,352 --> 00:08:12,654
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why this sutta, as I said, it's quite good because it actually shows you what the real problem is.

73
00:08:13,794 --> 00:08:21,936
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, like, you know, I'm sure many people would have read the sutta and it's... it can almost seem like somewhat self-explanatory as well.

74
00:08:22,936 --> 00:08:24,917
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense that, oh, yeah, okay, so...

75
00:08:26,412 --> 00:08:45,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Regarding is sankhāra, and the origin of sankhāra is feelings born of ignorance contact, and that's also impermanent, and then feelings born of ignorance contact give rise to craving, craving gives rise to that, and there it is, and I learned it.

76
00:08:46,868 --> 00:08:55,615
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, if you approach the whole thing as something that needs to be seen here and now, not just outlined as like a theoretical truth,

77
00:08:56,797 --> 00:09:04,682
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that the very act of appropriation, so that's what it means, like, regarding, is an activation.

78
00:09:06,303 --> 00:09:12,547
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the very act of appropriation is due to the craving.

79
00:09:15,188 --> 00:09:25,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when a puthujjana feels anything, and obviously, at any given time, he will feel, he will be feeling something, that pressure there,

80
00:09:27,361 --> 00:09:36,627
[SPEAKER_00]: There is felt that the pressure that endures, craving in regard to that, is his appropriation.

81
00:09:39,089 --> 00:09:44,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, obviously, the natural question is, OK, so how do I stop the craving?

82
00:09:44,732 --> 00:09:48,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, if you follow the theoretical truth, well, remove the ignorance.

83
00:09:48,615 --> 00:09:50,036
[SPEAKER_00]: And there you go, Abhidhamma answered it.

84
00:09:50,796 --> 00:09:52,998
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like, OK, so what is it that you do

85
00:09:54,682 --> 00:09:57,664
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is a pleasant feeling enduring.

86
00:09:58,445 --> 00:10:09,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, look at where does your motivation want to go, what are the general kind of... what is the general nature of those types of intentions in regard to that type of feeling.

87
00:10:10,335 --> 00:10:10,855
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, same.

88
00:10:11,415 --> 00:10:16,660
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's an unpleasant feeling, what is it that your mind revolves around?

89
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:20,803
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the main kind of defining attitude of that experience as a whole?

90
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:27,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's kind of... Well, that's the part that's being overlooked.

91
00:10:27,722 --> 00:10:28,883
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the craving is.

92
00:10:30,263 --> 00:10:40,588
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I can tell you, like, well, craving is something the animal does, but it's something that you don't see it as belonging to the animal, by default.

93
00:10:41,328 --> 00:10:43,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not like a...

94
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:47,791
[SPEAKER_00]: It can never be an object of your attention, something that you can just attend and perceive.

95
00:10:48,612 --> 00:10:51,173
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you can do two other things.

96
00:10:52,357 --> 00:10:54,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's all always overlooked.

97
00:10:56,360 --> 00:11:13,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you learn how to see craving as actually belonging to this living animal eye, the citta, then you won't appropriate it.

98
00:11:13,355 --> 00:11:14,176
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't stop it.

99
00:11:15,945 --> 00:11:26,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not that the craving will stop, but it will stop being yours, that specific phenomenon of craving in regard to the feelings born of ignorance pressure.

100
00:11:27,955 --> 00:11:34,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like I need to hunt down the craving in my experience and then stop doing it.

101
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:40,126
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like I'm seeing it and experiencing it every single day, left and right.

102
00:11:41,132 --> 00:11:46,233
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to this, in regard to that, in regard to pleasure, in regard to pain, in regard to neutral feeling.

103
00:11:47,493 --> 00:11:51,094
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the phenomenon that is always overlooked.

104
00:11:53,394 --> 00:12:00,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you see it, you cannot help but feel like it is yours.

105
00:12:04,836 --> 00:12:06,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's just how it feels.

106
00:12:07,497 --> 00:12:08,577
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like it's yours.

107
00:12:11,673 --> 00:12:13,214
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it that it feels like it's yours?

108
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:25,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as I said, pick up a... a... whatever you're currently feeling or, you know, if it's too subtle, bring up something unpleasant, something you don't like to think about.

109
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:29,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, look at the background of that.

110
00:12:29,901 --> 00:12:34,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how does... where does your mind lean towards or away from?

111
00:12:38,943 --> 00:12:41,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's not like something you can do once and get an immediate answer.

112
00:12:41,906 --> 00:12:50,473
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like uncovering the attitude of your citta in regard to whatever's being felt.

113
00:12:50,553 --> 00:12:53,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like... think of it like, I don't know, you have a dog on a leash.

114
00:12:55,077 --> 00:12:58,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, my favorite examples.

115
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:05,026
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a dog on the leash and it's just sitting or standing next to you, but it's not really pulling.

116
00:13:05,666 --> 00:13:06,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not pulling on the leash.

117
00:13:12,541 --> 00:13:20,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And then something comes, you know, in its field of vision, or it hears something, or it smells something, and it gets up, and it goes.

118
00:13:20,727 --> 00:13:23,649
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to go, and now you're feeling the tension in the leash.

119
00:13:29,054 --> 00:13:41,003
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that tension, in regard to whatever has been, you know, perceived, felt, intended, and so on, in regard to the aggregates, that is the craving.

120
00:13:42,052 --> 00:13:48,495
[SPEAKER_00]: But it, again, tension in and of itself, especially when you look at it in your own experience, it doesn't really have a subject, so to speak.

121
00:13:48,555 --> 00:13:50,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you can immediately see.

122
00:13:50,396 --> 00:13:53,198
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can tell to yourself, oh, this is my mind is doing it.

123
00:13:54,038 --> 00:14:04,363
[SPEAKER_00]: But in and of itself, the phenomenon of that, eh... tension within the pressure that's already there, that phenomenon is appropriated by default.

124
00:14:05,264 --> 00:14:08,886
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot not feel it, quote-unquote, as yours.

125
00:14:09,993 --> 00:14:28,309
[SPEAKER_00]: As something directly affecting me, something I have to deal with, either through allowing the dog to run after things, so the tension is released, or, you know, trying to get rid of it, or deal with it, or destroy it, or remove it, or deny it, whatever has kind of prompted it, so that the tension is released.

126
00:14:31,032 --> 00:14:35,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, for as long as you are acting out of that tension, one way or the other,

127
00:14:37,658 --> 00:14:39,359
[SPEAKER_00]: You are regarding it as self.

128
00:14:40,419 --> 00:14:41,839
[SPEAKER_00]: You are regarding it as mine.

129
00:14:44,900 --> 00:14:49,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, if it weren't yours, it wouldn't be a problem.

130
00:14:50,222 --> 00:14:54,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't worry about someone else's tensions, unless you feel affected by it.

131
00:15:00,271 --> 00:15:02,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you then stop being affected by it?

132
00:15:03,437 --> 00:15:04,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the gradual training.

133
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:10,030
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not going to start here again, because we spoke about it so many times, but the whole point of the gradual training is

134
00:15:11,169 --> 00:15:17,274
[SPEAKER_00]: To lead towards that recognition, that even that taṇha is the animal's doing.

135
00:15:17,514 --> 00:15:25,561
[SPEAKER_00]: It's literally the dog that has seen something, it's pulling, it's pulling, but you don't notice that the pulling and the tension you feel, it's because of the dog.

136
00:15:26,241 --> 00:15:27,022
[SPEAKER_00]: It is his doing.

137
00:15:27,122 --> 00:15:36,790
[SPEAKER_00]: You think it's because of what's been seen, what's been heard, what's been said, or what hasn't been said, or because you didn't get enough of this, or you had too much of something else that you didn't want, and so on.

138
00:15:37,710 --> 00:15:40,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Overlooking the obvious, that

139
00:15:42,566 --> 00:15:45,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling that arose a kind of ignorance pressure.

140
00:15:45,607 --> 00:15:48,328
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, ignorance pressure is just that, like not seeing the animal.

141
00:15:49,849 --> 00:15:55,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not even noticing that it's the dog that jumped up and started dragging you around.

142
00:15:55,851 --> 00:15:56,791
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not even seeing that.

143
00:15:56,871 --> 00:15:59,512
[SPEAKER_00]: You just see what the dog wants, basically.

144
00:15:59,532 --> 00:16:02,413
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only thing you notice and that's the only thing you're concerned with.

145
00:16:06,775 --> 00:16:09,576
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognizing that the craving is the doing of citta,

146
00:16:11,129 --> 00:16:29,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, truly recognizing, not just theoretically telling it to yourself, like, oh, this is not mine, but, like, genuinely seeing that, oh, there is a feeling arisen, and then there is that kick, or lean, or jump, and you are able to basically keep your distance from it as well, internally.

147
00:16:30,792 --> 00:16:34,655
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can only be developed through actually gradually training the animal.

148
00:16:34,695 --> 00:16:40,420
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no amount of information, meditation retreat, special insights that can just magically do that.

149
00:16:40,560 --> 00:16:45,044
[SPEAKER_00]: That space, you're literally carving out that space yourself through the gradual training.

150
00:16:46,705 --> 00:16:53,071
[SPEAKER_00]: And when there is enough of that space, then that kick, the scream, the bite of an animal is away from you.

151
00:16:53,191 --> 00:16:55,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not as felt as acutely as before.

152
00:16:58,817 --> 00:17:07,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And why this sutta is so interesting, is because you start carving out that space, you are undoing the wrong views.

153
00:17:07,213 --> 00:17:10,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even the wrong views, as we see further down,

154
00:17:12,483 --> 00:17:37,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, all types of... see, regarding formless self or self as possessing form or form in self or self in form, all of those are pretty much the symptoms and the permutations and the spilling out of... spillings, basically, of the unresolved craving that's still being appropriated.

155
00:17:39,220 --> 00:18:00,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could be philosophically bending your mind, like, endlessly, training it all you want, in terms of penetrating through the wrong views, and classifying them, and writing them down, and dividing them into subgroups, and learning about them, and making a whole network, and connecting all the dots and all the charts.

156
00:18:00,852 --> 00:18:05,354
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is... well, as the Sutta says, all of that is an activation.

157
00:18:06,775 --> 00:18:07,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Born out of what?

158
00:18:07,556 --> 00:18:23,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, born out of the same feeling, paired with ignorance pressure, that basically makes you overlook the citta, overlook its tendencies, overlook its direct tension in regard to pleasure, in regard to pain, in regard to neutral feeling, not dealing with it, not carving out that space.

159
00:18:24,892 --> 00:18:26,774
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is the result of that, basically.

160
00:18:27,817 --> 00:18:38,113
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to undo the wrong views, you want to undo the sense of self, you want to undo the level of your dependence on these possessions.

161
00:18:38,754 --> 00:18:40,616
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, well, you have to start

162
00:18:42,937 --> 00:18:44,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Training in regard to the present enduring feeling.

163
00:18:44,998 --> 00:18:52,902
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to start uprooting the... uprooting the... well, not cultivating, not maintaining the attitudes born out of that craving.

164
00:18:52,922 --> 00:19:02,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, sense restraint, virtue, is not just something you keep externally, but something you train in to get closer and closer to the recognition of that mind.

165
00:19:04,568 --> 00:19:06,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is what perpetuates the problem.

166
00:19:07,789 --> 00:19:09,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's allowing the dog

167
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:13,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, to pull you, or even like letting it loose from the leash.

168
00:19:13,422 --> 00:19:19,605
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just, just like, oh, go for it, because when you're, when I hold you with a leash, I feel the tension.

169
00:19:20,565 --> 00:19:24,367
[SPEAKER_00]: When I release it, I feel free and light and, and, and, and relieved.

170
00:19:24,487 --> 00:19:25,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, it is good.

171
00:19:27,108 --> 00:19:28,909
[SPEAKER_00]: And it isn't, and that's the problem.

172
00:19:30,129 --> 00:19:38,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just regarding the aggregates itself, but then as the, as the sutta continues, you could see even the view

173
00:19:40,123 --> 00:19:46,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody can actually... By the way, see, this is the wrong view now.

174
00:19:46,447 --> 00:19:52,571
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one of the annihilationist views that people often take as a caricature, thinking like, of course, nobody thinks this.

175
00:19:53,511 --> 00:19:54,272
[SPEAKER_00]: But think about it here.

176
00:19:54,352 --> 00:20:00,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's listed after somebody who is not regarding their aggregates as self anymore.

177
00:20:02,356 --> 00:20:07,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody obviously would have trained citta sufficiently

178
00:20:08,861 --> 00:20:22,512
[SPEAKER_00]: To not regard form, feeling, perception, activations of consciousness as self, means they have carved out some space, way more than your average Bhutujana, way more than your average Buddhist Bhutujana today.

179
00:20:27,397 --> 00:20:36,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, they might have a view that persists, which is either, well, after all this breaks apart,

180
00:20:38,759 --> 00:20:39,399
[SPEAKER_00]: I shall remain.

181
00:20:39,799 --> 00:20:48,362
[SPEAKER_00]: That sense of appropriation that has been inadvertently, wrongly maintained and cultivated is kind of assumed to continue.

182
00:20:50,123 --> 00:20:54,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, it's assumed that it won't continue, like the next paragraph.

183
00:20:55,005 --> 00:20:58,046
[SPEAKER_00]: But even those subtle views... Again, because somebody might be puzzled.

184
00:20:58,106 --> 00:20:59,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, how do I figure this out?

185
00:21:00,807 --> 00:21:01,467
[SPEAKER_00]: You might think that.

186
00:21:02,447 --> 00:21:04,328
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I actually figure this out?

187
00:21:05,108 --> 00:21:06,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, let me think about these views.

188
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:12,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And see... to understand them, to penetrate them.

189
00:21:12,683 --> 00:21:18,528
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like... you figure them out, by carving the space out away from the craving.

190
00:21:19,509 --> 00:21:31,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And you carve that space out by not acting out of craving, by not losing the sight of your citta, that you would have sufficiently seen, hopefully by then, through virtue, withdrawal from centrality, sense restraint and solitude.

191
00:21:32,581 --> 00:21:34,923
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see the movements within yourself,

192
00:21:35,764 --> 00:21:42,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards pleasure, towards pain, and you would... doesn't matter how much you appropriate it, the time will come when it will feel like I am not doing this.

193
00:21:43,772 --> 00:21:49,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what means like seeing the mind, seeing the signs of the mind, seeing its life independent of you.

194
00:21:50,498 --> 00:21:55,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like thinking about it theoretically or something.

195
00:21:56,785 --> 00:21:58,186
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what it means to see the mind.

196
00:22:01,109 --> 00:22:30,058
[SPEAKER_00]: And if somebody trained that and continues training it, even the view that, you know, this sense of self, this appropriation, that's kind of... not necessarily even bound with aggregates anymore, but it still has some standpoint in regard to it, like self and the world, like in the broadest sense, in the most general sense, all the peripheral, you know, the peripheral awareness and all the background, that's kind of regarded as mine.

197
00:22:33,418 --> 00:22:37,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that will fade away if the same training continues.

198
00:22:37,659 --> 00:22:44,340
[SPEAKER_00]: If the same taming of citta, of the same citta, of the same animal, in the same manner, just in regard to subtler things now, obviously.

199
00:22:46,321 --> 00:22:49,181
[SPEAKER_00]: And then this, eh, the... Where was the other view?

200
00:22:49,621 --> 00:22:50,462
[SPEAKER_00]: I scrolled past it.

201
00:22:51,062 --> 00:22:55,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, let me find it.

202
00:22:58,243 --> 00:22:59,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps, follow yourself.

203
00:23:00,884 --> 00:23:01,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, yeah, no, that was it.

204
00:23:03,230 --> 00:23:03,750
[SPEAKER_00]: There is, yeah.

205
00:23:04,470 --> 00:23:31,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neither that Self and Cosmos are the same, nor that somebody might not think that, might have even like, as I said, distanced themselves even from that peripheral and general awareness and the background of things being seen rightly, but they still might think that, oh, I might not be, might not be mine, so they're bringing, already bringing the sense of Self into question.

206
00:23:34,571 --> 00:23:38,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that they are even taking their sense of I am for granted anymore.

207
00:23:39,716 --> 00:23:47,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's generally sense of self is being fully brought to question, but that thing is not questioned.

208
00:23:48,573 --> 00:23:55,238
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what this... because the Buddha said in other suttas that this is like the highest of the right views that Putujana can get to.

209
00:23:55,538 --> 00:23:56,619
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the best he can get.

210
00:23:56,779 --> 00:23:57,780
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

211
00:23:57,800 --> 00:23:59,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I might not be, and he might not be mine.

212
00:23:59,461 --> 00:24:00,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not be.

213
00:24:00,302 --> 00:24:02,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he knows that he won't be permanent.

214
00:24:03,965 --> 00:24:15,053
[SPEAKER_00]: But the sense of that impermanence, the sense of that inevitable destruction, that is taken as something that you delight in.

215
00:24:16,583 --> 00:24:21,086
[SPEAKER_00]: However subtly, that is taken as as an external standpoint.

216
00:24:22,346 --> 00:24:34,133
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why even that view here, it says, means basically that kind of implies a degree of appropriation, a degree of ownership, which means they're going to be a degree of self-involved.

217
00:24:35,754 --> 00:24:37,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, how do I fix that?

218
00:24:38,056 --> 00:24:38,656
[SPEAKER_00]: The same way.

219
00:24:38,756 --> 00:24:40,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Continue training the same thing.

220
00:24:42,256 --> 00:24:43,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And, by the way, you can imagine.

221
00:24:44,017 --> 00:24:44,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about it.

222
00:24:44,557 --> 00:24:47,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you start keeping the precepts.

223
00:24:47,979 --> 00:24:51,440
[SPEAKER_00]: How will that feel from the point of view of somebody who doesn't keep the precepts?

224
00:24:53,582 --> 00:24:56,203
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, that's gonna feel like, well, how does it say?

225
00:24:56,243 --> 00:24:56,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Like an abyss.

226
00:24:58,024 --> 00:24:59,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a sudden precipice.

227
00:24:59,865 --> 00:25:03,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, you know, a celibacy will feel like a sudden precipice.

228
00:25:03,286 --> 00:25:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it says.

229
00:25:04,007 --> 00:25:05,467
[SPEAKER_00]: But, basically, yeah, precepts.

230
00:25:05,547 --> 00:25:08,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it stops feeling like that.

231
00:25:09,489 --> 00:25:15,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You train, you get closer, you maintain, you fail, you get back closer, da-da-da, you develop it, and it becomes a new base.

232
00:25:15,552 --> 00:25:17,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you train further now.

233
00:25:18,133 --> 00:25:20,794
[SPEAKER_00]: There are other things within that, that the mind wants.

234
00:25:21,095 --> 00:25:25,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe wants company, wants, eh... interactions, wants distractions.

235
00:25:25,497 --> 00:25:26,597
[SPEAKER_00]: You start to recognize that.

236
00:25:26,637 --> 00:25:29,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start to recognize those tensions, those pulls of the dog.

237
00:25:30,079 --> 00:25:31,039
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop fueling them.

238
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,441
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop losing sight of the dog.

239
00:25:34,281 --> 00:25:38,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So that you can eventually see it as the dog's doing, as citta's doing.

240
00:25:39,918 --> 00:25:44,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just tell it to yourself and adopt a view that this is cheetah's doing, therefore it's not my problem.

241
00:25:46,221 --> 00:25:50,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still affected by any pressure, by any tension?

242
00:25:50,424 --> 00:25:53,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there still a compulsion to act towards pleasure?

243
00:25:53,185 --> 00:25:57,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Compulsion to get away from pain means it is your doing.

244
00:25:57,427 --> 00:26:01,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Your regarding of it is still a self and that is an activation.

245
00:26:02,230 --> 00:26:02,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Born out of what?

246
00:26:02,950 --> 00:26:09,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, born out of that unchecked, untamed, still appropriated craving of the animal.

247
00:26:11,525 --> 00:26:24,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you work through that, and say now, the animal is not craving as much towards the feeling, because you've been training, you've been containing, you've been enduring things on the right level, as I said.

248
00:26:25,297 --> 00:26:33,364
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the internal space now that's discerned, so even when the animal jumps, you are not immediately, kind of, pulled with that jump.

249
00:26:34,188 --> 00:26:34,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize.

250
00:26:34,928 --> 00:26:35,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, it jumps.

251
00:26:35,889 --> 00:26:36,489
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down.

252
00:26:36,549 --> 00:26:36,869
[SPEAKER_00]: It jumps.

253
00:26:37,129 --> 00:26:38,029
[SPEAKER_00]: I remain untouched.

254
00:26:38,610 --> 00:26:44,552
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means to not suffer amid suffering, that I often say, and even the suttas, I think.

255
00:26:45,612 --> 00:26:47,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, basically, the suffering, i.e.

256
00:26:47,773 --> 00:26:54,715
[SPEAKER_00]: the animal, still has these acts and tendencies, but you are completely, like, separated from it.

257
00:26:55,805 --> 00:27:04,768
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no, as I said, that immediate pang of, like, when an unpleasant feeling arises or, like, unpleasant news or frightening perception.

258
00:27:05,249 --> 00:27:11,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is... the animal jumps, but you are not stabbed by it, to put it that way.

259
00:27:13,191 --> 00:27:15,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is... there is the space between...

260
00:27:15,352 --> 00:27:25,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how immediate, how fast, how unexpected feeling arises and how unexpected animal's reaction might be.

261
00:27:26,106 --> 00:27:29,329
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a space between all of that and your point of view, so to speak.

262
00:27:29,649 --> 00:27:31,611
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that internal carving of space that I said.

263
00:27:32,431 --> 00:27:45,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody would train rightly, somebody would then even undo these views, such as that, okay, well, this thing that I've developed, this most general thing, nothing specific anymore,

264
00:27:46,888 --> 00:27:47,629
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll continue.

265
00:27:47,869 --> 00:27:48,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You take that as self.

266
00:27:48,870 --> 00:27:49,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Or recognize, no, no.

267
00:27:50,191 --> 00:27:51,072
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the distraction.

268
00:27:51,292 --> 00:27:52,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything will come to pass.

269
00:27:53,173 --> 00:27:56,356
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you kind of take that as an external standpoint.

270
00:27:56,977 --> 00:27:59,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that would fade away, if you continue the training.

271
00:27:59,278 --> 00:28:02,681
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what you're left with is the undecided about the true dhamma.

272
00:28:02,721 --> 00:28:05,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you're kind of there, basically.

273
00:28:07,385 --> 00:28:10,308
[SPEAKER_00]: But there might be, now, the tendency to doubt

274
00:28:12,954 --> 00:28:15,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the unpleasant feeling.

275
00:28:15,796 --> 00:28:24,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead of enduring the doubt on the right level, as I often say, you justify addressing it.

276
00:28:25,424 --> 00:28:27,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Addressing it through the means of coping and managing.

277
00:28:28,266 --> 00:28:31,989
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, what does that addressing of doubt actually convey to you?

278
00:28:32,169 --> 00:28:38,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, convey that at least on that level, you still took for granted the arisen feeling,

279
00:28:39,225 --> 00:28:45,931
[SPEAKER_00]: There was unpleasant and you took for granted the attitude that you should get rid of it, resolve it and dispel the doubt.

280
00:28:47,272 --> 00:28:58,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And you stop doing that long enough, you will teach the dog to come down even in the core of the most existential, life-threatening doubt.

281
00:28:59,557 --> 00:29:01,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, by the way, it's not doubt about this or that.

282
00:29:02,398 --> 00:29:08,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I said, like, how would a puthujjana, non-celibate puthujjana, feel when he takes on the eight precepts?

283
00:29:08,081 --> 00:29:11,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the sutra says, it feels like an abyss, like a sudden precipice.

284
00:29:11,822 --> 00:29:27,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, how would somebody, who does find comfort, when the unpleasant feeling of impermanence, of nothing is in my control, nothing is sure, everything could be otherwise.

285
00:29:27,669 --> 00:29:28,549
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that feel?

286
00:29:29,550 --> 00:29:35,714
[SPEAKER_00]: For a putujana, or even for somebody who is who is maybe training and keeping the precepts, well, that feels like a new abyss now.

287
00:29:39,297 --> 00:29:45,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why these these views, like, enacting out of these views, it's not just kind of, oh, because people are confused.

288
00:29:45,441 --> 00:29:47,762
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because it's an existential coping.

289
00:29:48,883 --> 00:29:49,503
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to.

290
00:29:49,623 --> 00:29:50,964
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to cover that hole.

291
00:29:50,984 --> 00:29:52,145
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to cover that abyss.

292
00:29:52,185 --> 00:29:53,926
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to cover the block, that threat.

293
00:29:54,227 --> 00:29:54,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Mask it.

294
00:29:56,600 --> 00:30:07,189
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the view that self and the cosmos are one and same, you start questioning that view.

295
00:30:07,209 --> 00:30:08,290
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna feel like an abyss.

296
00:30:12,213 --> 00:30:19,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You might then come to terms that, okay, no, everything's coming to an end, and take that as a standpoint, and then start questioning that view.

297
00:30:21,015 --> 00:30:32,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, either way with those views, the reason why you would be doing it, holding the view, maintaining the view, is because, again, the underneath it all, there is an enduring feeling that you're resisting.

298
00:30:33,148 --> 00:30:37,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather that you are accepting the resistance of the citta as your own.

299
00:30:38,754 --> 00:30:41,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing that it cannot be yours because there isn't enough space.

300
00:30:44,123 --> 00:30:50,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when I say there isn't enough space between you and citta, means, like, I don't know, like, you don't, you're not even aware that you're holding the leash.

301
00:30:50,147 --> 00:30:51,128
[SPEAKER_00]: The leash is tied to you.

302
00:30:51,208 --> 00:30:53,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just see the dog moving, you're being dragged.

303
00:30:53,729 --> 00:30:54,990
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see anything else in between.

304
00:30:59,653 --> 00:31:02,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I say, you question that view.

305
00:31:02,735 --> 00:31:13,823
[SPEAKER_00]: You question that the self and cosmos are impermanent or that you question that, well, like, again, if you start finding pleasure that everything will end, find, finding comfort in that view, you start questioning that.

306
00:31:14,354 --> 00:31:14,894
[SPEAKER_00]: With the opposite.

307
00:31:16,755 --> 00:31:28,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Just so to... not again to find the answer whether it's true or not, but to stop taking for granted acting from the unpleasant feeling.

308
00:31:30,983 --> 00:31:34,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, by the way, pleasant feeling is also unpleasant in that sense.

309
00:31:34,325 --> 00:31:35,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant feeling towards sensuality,

310
00:31:37,151 --> 00:31:39,392
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't follow it, it's going to be felt unpleasantly.

311
00:31:39,472 --> 00:31:41,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Not getting enough of the pleasure is unpleasant.

312
00:31:42,092 --> 00:31:43,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either way, dukkha will be there.

313
00:31:44,573 --> 00:31:55,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learning how to not act out of it, because blindly accepting it, blindly following the direction that it offers, means regarding it as self.

314
00:31:56,116 --> 00:32:03,118
[SPEAKER_00]: That resistance, that tension, that leash pulling, means taking it up at face value, which means it is yours.

315
00:32:03,138 --> 00:32:05,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what you say to yourself at the end of the day.

316
00:32:07,095 --> 00:32:09,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, what you are left with is now.

317
00:32:09,277 --> 00:32:20,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you don't... you would have continued training and now these two possibilities of these two views, either that you will persist or you'll be destroyed, either way you find some solace in it.

318
00:32:21,066 --> 00:32:22,146
[SPEAKER_00]: You questioned it enough.

319
00:32:22,727 --> 00:32:23,848
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you left with now?

320
00:32:24,368 --> 00:32:28,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you go used to that abyss of questioning your deepest existential safety.

321
00:32:31,010 --> 00:32:34,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you're questioning it, but then you fall into an answer.

322
00:32:34,513 --> 00:32:37,036
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you also learn that you shouldn't fall into an answer.

323
00:32:37,136 --> 00:32:41,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're questioning it, answers present themselves and you don't take them.

324
00:32:42,381 --> 00:32:43,241
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you left with?

325
00:32:46,024 --> 00:32:51,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're left with absolute, complete, through and through, top to bottom doubt.

326
00:32:52,490 --> 00:32:53,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Existential doubt.

327
00:32:54,872 --> 00:32:55,453
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do now?

328
00:32:58,600 --> 00:33:14,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you feel justified in now explaining, resolving, plugging, covering up that doubt, dealing with it, you are the... well, you're falling into the type that's undecided about the true dhamma.

329
00:33:15,147 --> 00:33:25,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Came to the point of the existential doubt, but took that for granted and is trying to get rid of it, resolve it, act out of the feeling that's been regarded as self due to that craving that's not seen.

330
00:33:30,255 --> 00:33:32,156
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that will be now the new abyss.

331
00:33:33,556 --> 00:33:38,658
[SPEAKER_00]: The existential abyss of...

332
00:33:38,678 --> 00:33:41,079
[SPEAKER_00]: The existential abyss of... Oh!

333
00:33:44,680 --> 00:33:47,661
[SPEAKER_00]: I refuse to take any standpoint externally.

334
00:33:47,681 --> 00:33:50,802
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how satisfactory you might be in terms of

335
00:33:51,595 --> 00:34:02,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, explaining the world, explaining the structure, explaining this or that, whichever, you know, whichever background you come from, in terms of your information, it's like, I'm not taking that for granted.

336
00:34:02,818 --> 00:34:08,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I am actually choosing to be left with that doubt.

337
00:34:09,100 --> 00:34:15,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is actually the surest thing of an unpolluted kind that I can experience.

338
00:34:16,282 --> 00:34:17,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither here nor there.

339
00:34:19,580 --> 00:34:21,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, either mine or not mine, so to speak.

340
00:34:23,783 --> 00:34:24,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Ambiguous.

341
00:34:24,964 --> 00:34:26,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's not wanted.

342
00:34:26,465 --> 00:34:28,027
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's the most unpleasant thing.

343
00:34:28,607 --> 00:34:32,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's why I said in those other old talks, like, nibbāna is the ultimate uncertainty.

344
00:34:33,772 --> 00:34:36,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Cultivate uncertainty in regard to your aggregates, in regard to your feelings.

345
00:34:36,874 --> 00:34:38,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't act out of them and train.

346
00:34:38,935 --> 00:34:40,396
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like... that's... that's nibbāna.

347
00:34:40,516 --> 00:34:42,837
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the direct destruction of influxes.

348
00:34:43,517 --> 00:34:47,939
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the last bit here that's mentioned in the suttas, like, they're undecided about true dhamma.

349
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:51,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not because, again, they haven't figured it out.

350
00:34:51,942 --> 00:34:56,364
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because when that existential doubt, when they came to that point, well,

351
00:34:58,212 --> 00:34:59,713
[SPEAKER_00]: They chose to act out.

352
00:35:00,013 --> 00:35:01,013
[SPEAKER_00]: They felt justified.

353
00:35:01,293 --> 00:35:09,755
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't apply the same principle that brought them to that point, which is putting everything into question and not taking it for granted and enduring it on the higher level and not acting out of it.

354
00:35:11,635 --> 00:35:12,756
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that was like that.

355
00:35:13,416 --> 00:35:18,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's not because, you know, you didn't... you feel like this or like that.

356
00:35:18,277 --> 00:35:21,618
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because it's felt, like, properly, like the abyss.

357
00:35:23,348 --> 00:35:31,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in your core of your being, it feels like you're hanging over a cliff and things could go either way in any direction and you have no say over it.

358
00:35:33,032 --> 00:35:44,417
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's frightening and so on, but really, it's like the degree of suffering that would arise on account of being in that position is not determined by the cliff or by being on the cliff.

359
00:35:44,537 --> 00:35:50,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's determined by how much you still depend on regarding self as form and so on.

360
00:35:52,146 --> 00:35:58,430
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, how untamed your mind is, how hard your animal can still pull you, that is what is frightening.

361
00:35:58,470 --> 00:35:59,351
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the pain.

362
00:35:59,631 --> 00:36:01,212
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the dukkha of that experience.

363
00:36:01,732 --> 00:36:04,995
[SPEAKER_00]: If the animal is calm, it's the most liberating experience ever.

364
00:36:05,735 --> 00:36:06,916
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't owe anything.

365
00:36:07,336 --> 00:36:09,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody can owe anything to you.

366
00:36:09,678 --> 00:36:11,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody can do anything to you.

367
00:36:11,819 --> 00:36:13,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for anybody.

368
00:36:13,700 --> 00:36:16,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody exists for you in that sense, in terms of connection.

369
00:36:16,382 --> 00:36:20,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, it's the most... it's the lightest, the most freeing, the most...

370
00:36:20,785 --> 00:36:22,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncumbersome, unburdening experience ever.

371
00:36:23,588 --> 00:36:26,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why the Buddha and other suttas changed that last bit.

372
00:36:26,771 --> 00:36:29,253
[SPEAKER_00]: I might not be in a sense of, oh, it will not be.

373
00:36:30,034 --> 00:36:30,815
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't be for me.

374
00:36:30,875 --> 00:36:32,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if it were, it won't be for me, in other words.

375
00:36:32,817 --> 00:36:34,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he took that appropriation out of it.

376
00:36:35,659 --> 00:36:37,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of corrected it, so to speak.

377
00:36:38,382 --> 00:36:43,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that that sense of uncertainty, you know, as Ajahn Chah said, like, oh, not sure, not sure.

378
00:36:43,807 --> 00:36:44,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Just practice that.

379
00:36:45,566 --> 00:36:46,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, practice that.

380
00:36:46,487 --> 00:36:46,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Through and through.

381
00:36:47,108 --> 00:36:48,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just selectively.

382
00:36:48,550 --> 00:36:54,859
[SPEAKER_00]: When things suit you, or as a coping management technique, when you don't get your way, then you recollect.

383
00:36:54,919 --> 00:36:55,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

384
00:36:55,800 --> 00:36:56,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Not sure, not sure.

385
00:36:57,643 --> 00:36:58,023
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

386
00:36:58,143 --> 00:36:59,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Not sure in regard to everything.

387
00:37:00,066 --> 00:37:00,607
[SPEAKER_00]: No exception.

388
00:37:02,053 --> 00:37:07,637
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to your sense of safety, your health, your life, all these are the perceptions that the suttas talk about that you need to develop.

389
00:37:08,357 --> 00:37:11,239
[SPEAKER_00]: There was that, like, that... that Zen...

390
00:37:11,519 --> 00:37:14,141
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was, like, a Zen saying or some Zen monk wrote it.

391
00:37:14,701 --> 00:37:23,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, before the practice, mountains were mountains, rivers were rivers, and, I don't know, forests were forests, or something like that.

392
00:37:23,887 --> 00:37:29,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, during the practice, when he started practicing, like, mountains were not mountains, and forests were not forests.

393
00:37:30,171 --> 00:37:31,492
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he was doubting everything.

394
00:37:32,644 --> 00:37:42,029
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when the mind calmed down within that doubt, he didn't give up the training, he was like, oh, mountains were mountains again, forests were forests, after the successful practice.

395
00:37:43,850 --> 00:37:52,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like another, I think it was another Zen, maybe it was from the same monk, I don't know, when it was like, the practice kind of brings you to the point of... how do you say?

396
00:37:52,854 --> 00:37:58,197
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like, it feels like there is no sky above your head and no ground below your feet.

397
00:37:59,417 --> 00:38:21,466
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, like, yeah, the pretty much the last stage of everything is in doubt, everything is felt in that sense, and the only thing you can do is try and not take it at face value and follow that that need, that pressure to resolve it, to address it, to deal with it, and instead you choose to stay with it on that on that abyss level.

398
00:38:22,611 --> 00:38:25,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much kind of how you did from the start when you took on the precepts.

399
00:38:25,653 --> 00:38:26,854
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to take on the abyss.

400
00:38:26,994 --> 00:38:28,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, gradual training.

401
00:38:28,636 --> 00:38:30,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't slot into it overnight.

402
00:38:31,478 --> 00:38:47,470
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same practice of creating space between you and that reaction, automated reaction of the animal, the inevitable flinch of the animal that you cannot but not take as mine.

403
00:38:48,530 --> 00:38:50,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you don't see anything in between.

404
00:38:51,592 --> 00:39:05,883
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when craving is truly, correctly, when that movement, when that leaning of the animal is truly seen as, oh, like again, not your doing, you don't have to tell yourself, oh, this is not myself, therefore it's anatta.

405
00:39:07,345 --> 00:39:10,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already felt not as mine.

406
00:39:10,327 --> 00:39:15,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's already, eh... not going to be the dukkha.

407
00:39:16,952 --> 00:39:19,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the dukkha is when the craving is taken as yours.

408
00:39:20,630 --> 00:39:22,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why taṇha is the reason for dukkha.

409
00:39:24,774 --> 00:39:41,949
[SPEAKER_00]: When that taṇha, when that selfness of that craving, that regarding it as self, when the feeling arises, when that taṇha is not regarded as self, through right, correct, as I said, internal distancing, creating space between you and the citta,

410
00:39:43,505 --> 00:39:44,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha cannot touch you.

411
00:39:45,146 --> 00:39:53,069
[SPEAKER_00]: You could factually recognize, oh, this is unpleasant, and the animal is scared, or it's excited, or it wants to do this, wants to do this, but none of that is sucking you in.

412
00:39:53,729 --> 00:39:55,270
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that can get to you.

413
00:39:55,350 --> 00:40:01,453
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it's like seeing waves coming and knowing they will have to stop short of your feet.

414
00:40:02,273 --> 00:40:04,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you created enough space.

415
00:40:05,014 --> 00:40:07,115
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is... you might have doubted it initially.

416
00:40:07,135 --> 00:40:10,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Like Sotāpanna can maybe initially think, oh, maybe I could still suffer in some ways.

417
00:40:11,617 --> 00:40:18,480
[SPEAKER_00]: But repeatedly, actions, reactions of the citta cannot touch you and stop short of your feet.

418
00:40:18,920 --> 00:40:20,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how big the waves are.

419
00:40:21,561 --> 00:40:24,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you don't need to tell yourself to be confident about it.

420
00:40:24,503 --> 00:40:24,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be.

421
00:40:26,035 --> 00:40:45,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're experiencing it over and over again, that you cannot be touched anymore, because the reason for you being touched was taking that, as I said, ultimate, habitual resistance of the animal towards pleasure, towards pain, wanting pleasure, taking that itself, because there wasn't enough space between the two of you.

422
00:40:46,707 --> 00:40:51,549
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, seeing the signs of the Mayācitta is the prerequisite for being free from suffering.

423
00:40:51,569 --> 00:40:54,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how can you see the the space between you and the

424
00:40:55,555 --> 00:40:57,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Split-second reaction of the citta.

425
00:40:58,096 --> 00:41:02,338
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't even see the citta, that's not going to happen.

426
00:41:04,139 --> 00:41:14,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, if one wants to be free from doubt, as in overcome doubt as a fetter, the last bit, I often, I mean, I said that before in many other talks, you have to

427
00:41:16,376 --> 00:41:17,497
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, initially, you have to learn.

428
00:41:17,517 --> 00:41:18,779
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to collect the right information.

429
00:41:18,859 --> 00:41:20,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you need to clarify the doubt and so on.

430
00:41:21,061 --> 00:41:22,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that you're doubtful about.

431
00:41:22,263 --> 00:41:31,072
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point will come when you will recognize that you're doing it out of compulsion, doing it out of trying to regain that mental sense of kind of...

432
00:41:31,853 --> 00:41:35,696
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, satisfaction, safety, by removing that discomfort of the doubt.

433
00:41:35,756 --> 00:41:38,979
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not really anymore about information and things you're doubtful about.

434
00:41:39,419 --> 00:41:47,806
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about, as I said, taking for granted the attitude of trying to appease those uncomfortable feelings and replace them with something that citta deems more comfortable.

435
00:41:48,387 --> 00:41:49,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to free yourself from doubt?

436
00:41:50,229 --> 00:41:51,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop fueling that behavior.

437
00:41:53,268 --> 00:41:56,069
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you have to start training in regard to the uncertainty.

438
00:41:56,670 --> 00:42:06,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how irrational, how big those waves get, you still mustn't be giving in to those wrong principles of acting out of it and so on.

439
00:42:06,614 --> 00:42:07,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you go beyond doubt.

440
00:42:08,475 --> 00:42:12,657
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you're free from the fetter of doubt, i.e.

441
00:42:12,857 --> 00:42:18,160
[SPEAKER_00]: that's how you become a sotāpanna, while still not necessarily be completely free from the hindrance of doubt.

442
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:30,164
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you know, in regard to this or that, you are still accepting, acting out of doubt to some extent here and there, but not in that utmost, most general, core, fundamental, existential sense.

443
00:42:30,944 --> 00:42:44,468
[SPEAKER_00]: When your own being, where your own sense of self is brought into question, you stopped panicking, you stopped running away, you stopped trying to blindly cover it up, and you stopped acting out of trying to manage that uncertainty.

444
00:42:45,186 --> 00:42:50,750
[SPEAKER_00]: You stayed with it long enough and the dog had no choice but to calm down and sit down and stop craving against it.

445
00:42:51,231 --> 00:42:53,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you saw the space between the two.

446
00:42:58,356 --> 00:42:58,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

447
00:43:12,324 --> 00:43:19,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, look, I mean, quickly, I can mostly answer all of it, but why does the Buddha describe every self of you as saṅkhara?

448
00:43:20,491 --> 00:43:21,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Does he mean the one who's doing it?

449
00:43:22,012 --> 00:43:23,994
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, it's all probably apparent by now.

450
00:43:25,435 --> 00:43:27,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're not doing it directly, but you're doing it indirectly.

451
00:43:27,817 --> 00:43:35,904
[SPEAKER_00]: You are doing it through allowing the acts of... Oh, yeah, the camera is going.

452
00:43:36,244 --> 00:43:37,926
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it by allowing the acts of craving.

453
00:43:41,923 --> 00:43:52,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Fueling them, accepting them, going further out of them, adding more to them, you are maintaining the activation which is your sense of self, your rātavāda and everything else.

454
00:43:54,207 --> 00:44:00,348
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the second question was, why does the Buddha trace this activation back to ignorance in regard to feeling what is one ignorant of?

455
00:44:00,489 --> 00:44:01,849
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that craving that you're ignorant of.

456
00:44:02,469 --> 00:44:05,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, taking it for granted, eh...

457
00:44:08,743 --> 00:44:09,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

458
00:44:09,483 --> 00:44:29,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, taking for granted that craving is pretty much something that everybody starts overlooking, starts as being overlooked, and it takes the whole gradual training, which is why the Buddha, you know, explained it the way he did, to be able to come to it, to actually see it as something done by the animal, not by you.

459
00:44:32,988 --> 00:44:34,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And we answered the third one.

460
00:44:34,748 --> 00:44:39,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is abandoning explicit self-views insufficient if doubt and uncertainty still remains?

461
00:44:39,130 --> 00:44:44,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because it means that you are still maintaining the attitude, same attitude, in regard to the feeling.

462
00:44:47,472 --> 00:44:52,653
[SPEAKER_00]: When doubt and uncertainty is present, unpleasantly it's felt, and you are basically still

463
00:44:53,881 --> 00:45:06,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling justified in managing it, trying to get rid of it, acting out of it, and not seeing that as something that needs to be endured.

464
00:45:06,156 --> 00:45:10,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so we got extra questions that people sent.

465
00:45:12,668 --> 00:45:14,749
[SPEAKER_00]: One was the Sutta mentioned doubt and uncertainties.

466
00:45:15,309 --> 00:45:20,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Could it be something as a momentum of citta, which caused doubting, even if the right view?

467
00:45:21,050 --> 00:45:21,630
[SPEAKER_00]: It is, yeah.

468
00:45:21,830 --> 00:45:23,211
[SPEAKER_00]: It's of course, it's a momentum of citta.

469
00:45:23,251 --> 00:45:24,711
[SPEAKER_00]: The citta wants to get rid.

470
00:45:24,851 --> 00:45:26,852
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal wants to deal with the discomfort.

471
00:45:29,132 --> 00:45:40,775
[SPEAKER_00]: And whether that discomfort is presented as precepts or the world that I thought will last forever, where I felt safe, will come to end,

472
00:45:42,230 --> 00:45:47,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, presented as nothing is certain, including the core of my being, it's discomfort of citta.

473
00:45:50,877 --> 00:46:03,527
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you are just blindly fueling the momentum of the animal that wants to get rid of the discomfort, you are then implicating yourself in everything else, basically.

474
00:46:05,974 --> 00:46:08,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what this question said later, yeah.

475
00:46:08,356 --> 00:46:15,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of the uncompromised following of the direction of the right view means internally abandoning any safety in the world.

476
00:46:15,642 --> 00:46:23,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Any safety in the world and then any safety in a sense of dealing with uncertainty and feeling justified about it.

477
00:46:24,870 --> 00:46:27,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I said that before, like, uncertainty, it's not really like...

478
00:46:28,452 --> 00:46:35,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not painful in a, you know, in a kind of unbearable sense, you know, like, I don't know, somebody burning you with fire or stabbing you with knives.

479
00:46:35,780 --> 00:46:39,744
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you can't, you know, the brain just shuts down from pain.

480
00:46:39,764 --> 00:46:40,905
[SPEAKER_00]: You just go unconscious.

481
00:46:41,125 --> 00:46:41,266
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

482
00:46:42,287 --> 00:46:48,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not actually unpleasant, yet it's the most unpleasant thing there is.

483
00:46:48,293 --> 00:46:50,976
[SPEAKER_00]: That mental uncertainty.

484
00:46:52,367 --> 00:46:53,508
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels far worse.

485
00:46:54,229 --> 00:47:03,716
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, not because it's objectively unpleasant, but because all the assumptions, needs and habits and lack of training of the animal and the pressure it puts on you because of it.

486
00:47:04,276 --> 00:47:05,017
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

487
00:47:08,140 --> 00:47:10,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how does one know right view is truly right view?

488
00:47:10,902 --> 00:47:18,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when, as I said, when the waves cannot get to you anymore.

489
00:47:19,360 --> 00:47:26,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you found a perfect response to every wave, but because from where you're looking at them, it's simply untouchable.

490
00:47:27,222 --> 00:47:42,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, when you have trained your citta sufficiently enough, you will know the right view as the right view, because you will not be able to suffer amidst the core of suffering, which was the the the quick animal reaction to pain or pleasure or neutral feeling.

491
00:47:44,754 --> 00:47:49,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the twitch, the subconscious reaction, to put it that way.

492
00:47:50,800 --> 00:47:52,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that cannot touch you.

493
00:47:52,642 --> 00:47:55,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you know, oh, yeah, the right view is the right view.

494
00:47:55,686 --> 00:48:00,391
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, amid suffering, I cannot be touched by suffering.

495
00:48:00,411 --> 00:48:04,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, I would still prefer that the animal is calmer, therefore I should continue practicing.

496
00:48:04,975 --> 00:48:07,338
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, in its core,

497
00:48:08,777 --> 00:48:17,782
[SPEAKER_00]: There is, as I said, the space has been carved out and now it's like you cannot go back to what was before, which was you being confused.

498
00:48:18,283 --> 00:48:36,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Who does the craving and what and just automatically regarding those attitudes as yours because you were prioritizing through your own views, getting rid of the pain as soon as possible, prolonging pleasure as long as possible and kind of ignoring and distracting yourself from neutral feeling as quickly as that was presented.

499
00:48:40,628 --> 00:48:45,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Why was the translation of activation chosen?

500
00:48:45,390 --> 00:48:56,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because it kind of conveys, at least in part, a degree of your own doing.

501
00:48:59,625 --> 00:49:07,973
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, all the other... all the other kind of translations, formation, even determinations, is just too abstract.

502
00:49:08,313 --> 00:49:11,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, activation is chosen because it's... it's... it comes from activities.

503
00:49:13,938 --> 00:49:23,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of your perpetual taking up, engagement, proliferation of activities rooted in pursuing pleasure,

504
00:49:24,738 --> 00:49:33,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting rid of the discomfort, ignoring the neutral feeling, your sense of self is there, active.

505
00:49:33,944 --> 00:49:35,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Asavas are there, active.

506
00:49:35,505 --> 00:49:36,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha is there, active.

507
00:49:36,906 --> 00:49:37,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It's activated.

508
00:49:38,727 --> 00:49:39,147
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why.

509
00:49:40,128 --> 00:49:43,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to try and kind of...

510
00:49:45,909 --> 00:49:54,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Re-establish, basically, that connection to, you know, your own practice, like living, and things that you do, and choices that you make.

511
00:49:54,314 --> 00:49:55,855
[SPEAKER_00]: That is why these things are there.

512
00:49:57,315 --> 00:50:07,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it's just all formation and the form, it's kind of presumes, oh, it's something that happened back then, and it was formed, and therefore I must find some magical insight to unform it, or to find a way out of it.

513
00:50:07,301 --> 00:50:08,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, no, you are maintaining it.

514
00:50:10,402 --> 00:50:17,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why this sutta was so good, because it lists these views that people hold, you know, for the rest of their lives or lifetimes.

515
00:50:17,428 --> 00:50:20,730
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in many ways, you kind of perpetuate the same stuff.

516
00:50:21,331 --> 00:50:25,574
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of your activities in regard to what's currently felt.

517
00:50:26,375 --> 00:50:33,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking those activities for granted, following them, proliferating them, that's why everything else is there.

518
00:50:34,562 --> 00:50:36,223
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole mass of suffering, basically.

519
00:50:44,233 --> 00:50:54,329
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the center-self, that's how dukkha, that's how craving, that's how asavas are kept together, operating, affecting you.

520
00:50:55,251 --> 00:50:55,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why.

521
00:51:03,790 --> 00:51:04,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Got another question?

522
00:51:05,091 --> 00:51:06,531
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll add two parts to this question.

523
00:51:06,611 --> 00:51:16,934
[SPEAKER_00]: When deeply contemplating impermanence, a sense of existential dread arises questioning what the point of it all is, and sometimes it brings up a lot of humility, but it feels like conceit as well.

524
00:51:17,214 --> 00:51:19,675
[SPEAKER_00]: How does one work with both these states that arise?

525
00:51:21,432 --> 00:51:21,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, same.

526
00:51:21,953 --> 00:51:34,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, through questioning, depending on what state arises, you want to then see what is your current intention in regard to that state, in regard to that thing that are arising on account of certain contemplations.

527
00:51:34,979 --> 00:51:37,960
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep saying, that's why you always have to keep your motivation in check.

528
00:51:41,602 --> 00:51:47,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is, like, existential dread starts arising, what is it that the mind wants to do in regard to that?

529
00:51:49,785 --> 00:51:50,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you contemplate that now.

530
00:51:52,706 --> 00:51:53,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Is he trying to get rid of it?

531
00:51:55,027 --> 00:51:55,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

532
00:51:55,428 --> 00:52:02,171
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean... And actually ask yourself, like, when there is, like, anxiety or existential dread or anything, why is it a problem?

533
00:52:02,391 --> 00:52:09,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you were to ask yourself, what if I... what if this were to last forever, this... this feeling of uncertainty?

534
00:52:10,014 --> 00:52:13,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it a problem for me to stay with it for the rest of my life?

535
00:52:16,417 --> 00:52:18,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's not stopping you from living,

536
00:52:20,022 --> 00:52:21,323
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not stopping you from eating.

537
00:52:23,865 --> 00:52:27,308
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not stopping you from breathing, drinking water, doing stuff.

538
00:52:27,548 --> 00:52:28,609
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not stopping you from anything.

539
00:52:29,309 --> 00:52:30,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, I must deal with it.

540
00:52:31,491 --> 00:52:31,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

541
00:52:35,074 --> 00:52:36,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Where does that imperative come from?

542
00:52:38,256 --> 00:52:41,659
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, so how about I don't deal with it?

543
00:52:42,359 --> 00:52:43,320
[SPEAKER_00]: How about I live with it?

544
00:52:44,421 --> 00:52:45,642
[SPEAKER_00]: How about I learn to live with it?

545
00:52:48,898 --> 00:52:49,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's too much.

546
00:52:49,618 --> 00:52:49,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

547
00:52:50,139 --> 00:52:52,340
[SPEAKER_00]: How about learn to live with it a bit longer?

548
00:52:53,460 --> 00:52:55,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Not immediately just try to get rid of it.

549
00:53:01,064 --> 00:53:02,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you tame the citta.

550
00:53:02,878 --> 00:53:18,108
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the ten perceptions of impermanence, loss and so on that the suttas talk about, that one should be frequently bringing up to their mind, provided, you know, the mind is already properly fenced off with established virtue and sense restraint.

551
00:53:18,208 --> 00:53:23,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just... you're still acting out of body and speech coarsely and then start contemplating these things.

552
00:53:23,412 --> 00:53:27,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's probably gonna overwhelm.

553
00:53:27,915 --> 00:53:31,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind will probably overwhelm you because, like, literally you got a wild elephant

554
00:53:32,990 --> 00:53:40,932
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't really fastened it and secured it in a pen area where you are safe, and you start agitating it.

555
00:53:41,012 --> 00:53:41,912
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think will happen?

556
00:53:46,214 --> 00:53:47,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna go after you.

557
00:53:47,914 --> 00:53:51,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, again, the whole gradual training is the way it is.

558
00:53:52,735 --> 00:54:01,778
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, if the animal feels like it's getting close to you, in your own mind, in a threatening manner, that's why we say, well, you gotta go back and check

559
00:54:02,682 --> 00:54:03,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Are your precepts leaking?

560
00:54:04,042 --> 00:54:05,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Is your restraint not established?

561
00:54:05,543 --> 00:54:06,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still overlooking stuff?

562
00:54:07,583 --> 00:54:09,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is what gives the power to the citta.

563
00:54:10,744 --> 00:54:14,064
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what what kind of allows it to come close.

564
00:54:17,585 --> 00:54:30,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it might be scary, kicking and screaming, but if those... if the precepts are rightly trained in, intentions like habitually being revealed, not acting out of,

565
00:54:31,391 --> 00:54:33,653
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a fast, that is a solid anchor.

566
00:54:35,114 --> 00:54:36,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So the animal can be foaming in its mouth.

567
00:54:37,957 --> 00:54:41,740
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the length of the chain and you know it can't get you.

568
00:54:44,102 --> 00:54:48,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And as long as you don't stupidly reach out in its domain, you're safe.

569
00:54:49,166 --> 00:54:51,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's like right in your face.

570
00:54:53,790 --> 00:54:56,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it comes down and then starts to listen to you.

571
00:54:57,933 --> 00:54:58,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But not before that.

572
00:54:59,785 --> 00:55:02,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not gonna magically just suddenly obey you.

573
00:55:04,786 --> 00:55:05,466
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, why would it?

574
00:55:05,786 --> 00:55:11,167
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been feeding its whims for, well, this lifetime that you know, but, you know, many, many lifetimes before.

575
00:55:11,527 --> 00:55:14,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that needs to be undone.

576
00:55:15,528 --> 00:55:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: And the kind of fortunate thing with human situation, according to the suttas, is that even in this relatively short lifespan of your average human life,

577
00:55:28,502 --> 00:55:35,349
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the most optimal time, the most optimal place to undo eons of bad habits of the citta.

578
00:55:35,649 --> 00:55:36,430
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, you can...

579
00:55:36,870 --> 00:55:41,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eons of feeding the wild animal, you could, in 20 years, calm it down.

580
00:55:41,214 --> 00:55:43,517
[SPEAKER_00]: In these 20 years on this earth, you could calm it down.

581
00:55:44,998 --> 00:55:52,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the effort made in the human life is disproportionately effective than the effort to practice dhamma made in other realms.

582
00:55:56,275 --> 00:56:03,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was like that sutta where those devas were living together on a... like a... almost like on a... on a... on a tip of a needle.

583
00:56:03,821 --> 00:56:05,322
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how little space they have.

584
00:56:05,383 --> 00:56:07,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, they live in complete harmony and joy.

585
00:56:07,965 --> 00:56:12,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Sariputta thought, oh, it's because they're keeping their minds in practice up there, and keep their minds lofty.

586
00:56:12,829 --> 00:56:13,689
[SPEAKER_00]: And they said, no, no, no.

587
00:56:15,491 --> 00:56:22,016
[SPEAKER_00]: We live here harmoniously, because when we were humans, we made the... the... the right effort to practice dhamma to some extent.

588
00:56:22,497 --> 00:56:24,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, we're bearing the fruit of that.

589
00:56:25,776 --> 00:56:26,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in... in...

590
00:56:27,777 --> 00:56:28,098
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

591
00:56:28,118 --> 00:56:29,358
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't even specify the time.

592
00:56:29,619 --> 00:56:30,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the camera's gone again.

593
00:56:30,960 --> 00:56:47,871
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't even specify the time, how long they've been practicing as humans, but the point was... even if it was for a few years, basically, they practiced rightly, they tamed the mind rightly, and now, for eons, they're living in the, like, celestial pleasures.

594
00:56:48,591 --> 00:56:49,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was kind of

595
00:56:51,358 --> 00:56:56,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Indicative of the amount of result you get for the effort made in the human realm.

596
00:56:59,266 --> 00:57:00,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Right effort made.

597
00:57:00,727 --> 00:57:04,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And, then you can also extrapolate the opposite, the wrong effort made.

598
00:57:06,794 --> 00:57:08,155
[SPEAKER_00]: The amount of bad results you get.

